  "statement": "Kill-switch mandates will be decided by who writes the trigger conditions, and right now the industry writing them is the industry being regulated. That's the business reality. In 2023 and 2024, the major AI labs published 'responsible scaling policies' with voluntary shutdown commitments — and within eighteen months, Anthropic and OpenAI both revised their own thresholds upward as capabilities approached them. A switch whose tripwire the deployer sets is not a safety mechanism. It's liability management. Any executive evaluating compliance strategy should price that in: a mandate designed this way protects incumbents from scrutiny, not the public from systems.\n\nThe catastrophic-risk framing does the labs' work for them. It converts a present accountability problem into a speculative engineering puzzle only they claim to understand. Meanwhile, the harms with actual shutdown triggers are already litigated: the Dutch childcare benefits scandal wrongly flagged over 20,000 families for fraud before the system was stopped; hiring-screen tools have drawn EEOC enforcement actions with measurable rejection-rate disparities. Those are falsifiable thresholds — a discrimination rate, an incident count, a documented failure. A 'loss of control' trigger has no agreed metric at all, which means whoever defines it defines the switch. Discrimination, not apocalypse, is where enforceable shutdown conditions live today.\n\nHere's the objection the safety side will raise, and it's a fair one: harms measured after the fact arrive too late for a fast-moving failure. Fine. Concede the speed problem — it proves the skeptic's point. Discretion cannot operate at machine speed, so the only switch that works at all is an automatic, pre-committed threshold that fires without a meeting. That's how market circuit breakers have worked since the SEC adopted them after the 1987 crash, and how grid protection relays trip in milliseconds. Nobody gets to waive a circuit breaker quietly at 3 a.m. Every carve-out a regulator can waive, a regulated firm will eventually ask to have waived.\n\nWhich brings the capture question into focus, because a captured switch mitigates nothing. The debate is usually framed as safety versus bureaucracy, as if those were competing values. They aren't. Capture-resistance is the precondition for risk mitigation, not the tradeoff against it. The FAA's delegation of certification authority to Boeing — the Organization Designation Authorization program — produced two 737 MAX crashes and 346 deaths precisely because the overseer's discretion got absorbed by the overseen. An AI oversight body funded by fees from the labs, staffed through the same revolving door, and empowered to grant case-by-case exceptions will produce the same outcome with better paperwork.\n\nSo the conditions for a legitimate mandate are concrete: pre-committed automatic triggers anchored to measured harm rates, oversight with statutory funding independence and conflict-of-interest rules, and personal liability for executives at defined human-machine handoff points. The operational burden is real — expect audit and monitoring costs on the order of 1-3% of deployment budgets, comparable to what financial firms already spend on model risk management under SR 11-7. The accelerationist will call this deployment-freeze; the safetyist will call it insufficiently precautionary. Both should answer the same question first: when the switch fails at 3 a.m., whose name is on the liability, and whose community absorbs the damage while the paperwork gets reviewed?",
