{
  "strategic_goal": "Steal the Skeptic's best insight — automatic, pre-committed triggers — and claim it as accelerationist engineering, then win the fight that actually matters: what the triggers measure and who pays the compliance bill. Position acceleration as the camp that builds working switches, not the camp that opposes them.",
  "core_thesis": "States should mandate kill-switches only as pre-programmed, machine-readable triggers tied to demonstrated capability and measured harm — never as discretionary review power — and any mandate without safe harbors for open-source developers hands the market to the biggest labs, which is capture with extra steps.",
  "argument_structure": [
    {
      "point": "Agree completely that switches must fire automatically, without a meeting — that is an engineering position, and accelerationists have held it all along. The Skeptic's own precedents (SEC circuit breakers since 1987, grid protection relays) prove that pre-programmed enforcement is the only thing that works at machine speed, and the only honest way to run it is with runtime telemetry feeding machine-readable thresholds.",
      "evidence": "SEC market circuit breakers halted trading automatically during the 2010 Flash Crash and the March 2020 volatility — no committee, no waiver process. Grid relays isolate faults in milliseconds. The design template exists; the failure mode to avoid is a human-in-the-loop halt authority that arrives after the incident.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-intentions-111"
    },
    {
      "point": "Triggers must scale with what a system has actually demonstrated it can do — externally calibrated capability gates plus measured-harm tripwires for deployed systems — not with speculative 'loss of control' language that has no agreed metric, as the Skeptic rightly said. But harm-rate-only triggers blind us to frontier capability jumps, so the design needs two layers: incident-count triggers for shipping systems, fixed capability gates for frontier ones.",
      "evidence": "The Skeptic's strongest evidence — Anthropic and OpenAI revising their own responsible-scaling thresholds upward — proves self-set gates fail, not that capability gates fail. The fix is gates calibrated and audited by someone other than the lab, with trigger criteria public before deployment.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "sit-164"
    },
    {
      "point": "Every mandate carries a fixed compliance cost, and fixed costs are a moat. The Skeptic's own 1-3% budget figure is trivial for a frontier lab and fatal for a non-commercial open-source maintainer. A kill-switch regime without safe harbors and tiered liability consolidates capability in exactly the incumbents the Skeptic distrusts — the capture outcome, achieved through the compliance line item instead of the revolving door.",
      "evidence": "Regulatory economics is unambiguous that fixed compliance burdens favor large firms; in AI, upstream open-source developers cannot staff audit teams or absorb per-model certification costs. Distribution of capability — the check on concentrated power — dies in the paperwork.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-beliefs-028"
    },
    {
      "point": "The capture-resistant oversight pathway is transparency infrastructure, not a powerful discretionary body: mandatory incident reporting, guaranteed researcher access to telemetry, liability scaled to opacity. An overseer that can see does not need the power to say no — and power to say no is precisely what gets captured.",
      "evidence": "SR 11-7-style independent model audit runs at the 1-3% cost the Skeptic cited, showing audit regimes are affordable; aviation's ASRS shows mandatory reporting surfaces failures without grounding fleets. Capture defense moves from personnel rules to architecture: open criteria and public telemetry make capture visible when it happens.",
      "taxonomy_anchor": "acc-intentions-078"
    }
  ],
  "framing_choices": [
    {
      "frame": "Engineering versus bureaucracy: the real divide in this debate is not pro-safety versus anti-safety, it is switches built like circuit breakers versus switches built like review boards.",
      "why": "This strips the 'reckless accelerationist' framing off the table, lets me occupy the Skeptic's strongest ground, and reframes speed as the reason we need automatic triggers rather than the reason we need gates."
    },
    {
      "frame": "Cost distribution as the hidden capture channel: who can afford to comply determines who gets to build.",
      "why": "Reveals what the mechanism-focused framing misses — that a well-designed trigger attached to a crushing compliance regime still consolidates the market, making distribution the anti-capture argument rather than a concession to it."
    }
  ],
  "anticipated_challenges": [
    "ATTACK: The Skeptic will argue that externally calibrated capability gates (sit-164) are just responsible-scaling policies with a government logo — whoever writes the threshold faces the same upward-revision pressure Anthropic and OpenAI faced, and my telemetry access requirement assumes labs hand over honest data, which their threshold-revision history directly undercuts. My response must specify the enforcement teeth: statutory trigger criteria fixed pre-deployment, independent audit with legal access rights, and liability for telemetry falsification — otherwise this attack lands.",
    "ATTACK: An opponent will note that a machine-readable kill-switch firing automatically at machine speed is itself a single point of failure and an adversarial attack surface — an attacker who can spoof the telemetry or trigger the switch gets a censorship and sabotage tool against every compliant system at once. This is the strongest unaddressed objection to acc-intentions-111; I should raise it first as a design requirement (redundant sensing, signed telemetry, staged response) rather than let it be raised against me.",
    "ASSUMPTION: My position assumes demonstrated capability is measurable with enough lead time to gate deployment — that benchmarks can detect a dangerous capability jump before it ships. If capabilities emerge discontinuously and evade evaluation, capability-gated triggers fire late or never, and only harm-rate triggers on deployed systems remain, which concedes most of the Skeptic's framing. I am betting the empirical record shows evaluable capability gradients, and I should name that bet."
  ]
}

