tems, and tiering should accommodate them. But the tiering variable must be capability and replication potential, not revenue, because replication potential is what determines whether any state's switch can bind anyone.\n\nThe third condition is teeth. The Skeptic named personal liability at handoff points and left the enforcement mechanism blank. A waived or bypassed switch must cost a named officer personally and the firm structurally. The precedents are working today: Sarbanes-Oxley officer certification made executives sign for financial controls in 2002, and deferred prosecution agreements force monitored structural reform rather than token fines. Reserve the severest sanction — dissolution — for one case only: disabling or defying a mandated switch. Narrowed that way, it is not a death penalty for AI development; it is the price of tampering with the smoke detector. The objection that mandatory reporting becomes checkbox ritual is fair, since software CVEs and medical device recalls show chronic gaming. The answer is pairing disclosure with falsification liability and periodic outcome audits of the triggers themselves, not reporting alone.\n\nBoth speakers treated automatic triggers and regulator discretion as mutually exclusive. Deployed systems need both. Automatic floors handle known failure modes; a liability-shielded halt authority handles the novel ones no pre-committed threshold anticipated. The Accelerationist's own scenario — spoofed telemetry firing a false halt — concedes the governance layer is an attack surface that needs hardening. The FAA's delegation failure shows what transparency without authority produces; its airworthiness directives show that precautionary halt power works when halting is legally protected and waiving is personally costly. The business bottom line for any executive in this room: the consensus architecture is sound, but it currently rests on unverified sensors, unbounded distribution, and unpriced defiance. Build the reporting floor, tie obligations to replication potential, and make switch-defiance the one uninsurable offense — or the switch everyone just agreed to build will not exist when it is needed.",
