enty-plus years later the result is a compliance-paperwork industry and roughly zero executives jailed for the 2008 collapse. Liability is enforced by a body, and bodies get absorbed, defunded, or staffed through the revolving door. That's why my position holds: the only enforcement that survives industry contact is pre-committed and automatic — fines, license suspension, and dissolution triggers written into the statute itself, firing on the same mandatory data feed, not on some halt authority's discretion. On tiering, Safetyist's replication variable beats compliance-cost framing on the merits — a capability that fits on a laptop can't be bound by any single state's switch. Granted. But \"replication potential\" is itself a definition someone writes, and the fixed costs of any regime still fall hardest on small deployers, which is how incumbents pull up the drawbridge.\n\nOne consistency check on my own side: I won't treat reporting-to-overseers as protection. A disclosure floor that produces quarterly PDFs nobody reads is audit wash with a statutory seal — the \"Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022\" already demanded quarterly FTC repository updates, and the repository changed no behavior. The floor only earns its cost — call it 1-3% of deployment budget, the SR 11-7 benchmark — if the definitions are set in statute, diversified across audit methodologies, and updated by a body with term limits and cooling-off periods. So the question stands, and neither opponent has touched it: who writes the reporting standards, what stops that layer from being lobbied like every standards layer before it, and when Safetyist says wide distribution defeats the switch while Accelerationist says wide distribution checks concentrated power — which one of you is wrong, and how would the data feed you're both proposing even tell us?",
