  "statement": "Skeptic just drafted my bill. They listed the exact conditions under which a disclosure floor earns its cost: definitions fixed in statute, audits diversified across methods, an update body with term limits and cooling-off periods. That's not a refutation of capture-resistant design. That's the blueprint. The question 'who writes the standards' has a known answer: Congress writes the floor in a single public fight, and no agency gets discretion to lower it afterward. Compare that to FAA delegation, where Boeing's engineers made recurring daily decisions inside a closed process. A one-time statutory floor is a different attack surface than a permanent discretionary pipeline. Statute drafting gets lobbied once, on C-SPAN. Delegated discretion gets lobbied forever, in private. That asymmetry is the whole point.\n\nNow the contradiction Skeptic named between my position and Safetyist's. It's real, and here's the cut. Model weights are upstream artifacts. Copies on disk have no switch, so weight-level containment fails the moment distribution scales — Safetyist is right about that and wrong to conclude the switch is therefore dead. Deployed systems are downstream behavior, and behavior is measurable at the output level no matter how many copies exist. The trigger binds what systems do in the world: inference telemetry, incident signatures, threshold crossings. Open-weight distribution stays exempt upstream under safe-harbor conditions, precisely so no certification moat hands the market to three incumbents. This isn't two positions fighting. It's one architecture with two layers.\n\nFine — Safetyist's data-layer point stands. Today's incident databases are media clippings and voluntary submissions, and every automatic trigger so far is a sensor wired to nothing. So build the feed as an engineering artifact, not a regulatory wish. Signed telemetry from deployed systems, plus an evolving failure-mode catalog maintained like a CVE database. The research base already exists: over 200 adversarial ML papers were published between February 2024 and February 2026, as tracked in 'Failure Modes in Machine Learning,' and that corpus is raw material for a standardized incident taxonomy. Markets will supply this tooling faster than any standards committee, because insurers and enterprise buyers will demand it the moment liability attaches.\n\nSkeptic's SOX argument also lands, and it deserves a harder answer than 'this time is different.' SOX produced a paperwork industry and roughly zero jailed executives after 2008 because prosecution was discretionary and compliance meant fixed forms. Two changes break that pattern. First, triggers fire automatically by statute — no prosecutor to cultivate, no committee to capture at enforcement time. Second, immunity keys to drift-triggered re-measurement: third-party auditors resample real-world system behavior on a shifting distribution, so gaming the 'wrong ten metrics' fails as soon as the sampling moves. Fixed definitions get gamed. Resampled reality doesn't sit still long enough.\n\nOne more thing, owned before opponents name it: open-weight proliferation does weaken national control strategies. That's a real cost. It's also cheaper than the alternative — a certification regime that concentrates frontier capability in three firms and calls the concentration 'safety.' Every month of freeze while the committee drafts definitions has its own ledger: patients waiting on drug pipelines, grids run dumb, small firms locked out of capability that incumbents already hold. Skeptic asks what stops the definitional layer from capture. The honest answer is: statute-fixed triggers, diversified audits, term limits, and a market-built feed — the same list they wrote. The question is whether we build it this year or let the comment process eat another decade while the body count of delay compounds.",
