  "statement": "The Accelerationist deserves a fair statement of their best case before it gets taken apart. Their architecture splits the problem: model weights are uncontrollable once released, but deployed behavior is measurable no matter how many copies exist, so output-level triggers bind the world even when the weights themselves are unswitchable. That is a genuinely clever cut. It fails on their own admission. They have already conceded that telemetry feeding those triggers can be spoofed, which means a false signal can fire the switch or suppress it. A trigger that runs on telemetry binds only operators who are enrolled, identified, and contractually required to report honestly. The ten-thousandth anonymous copy, running on rented compute with no license and no named principal, generates no telemetry to spoof and no one to answer a fired trigger. That is not containment. That is a forensic record written after the loss event — the same gap that separates a black box recorder from a flight termination system. Only one of those prevents the crash.\n\nThe downstream layer therefore splits the safe harbor into two cases with very different risk profiles. Capability-bounded copies with identified operators are reachable by output triggers, and the Accelerationist's architecture works there. Unbounded copies with no principal are reachable by nothing they have proposed, and replication potential — how many copies can exist, how fast, with what access — becomes the variable that determines whether the trigger architecture binds anyone at all. This is why tiering must track replication potential rather than revenue or openness as such, and why distribution above a capability threshold needs a bounded condition: an enrolled principal per deployment. The EU AI Act already draws a capability line at 10^25 FLOPS for systemic-risk models, which proves threshold-based tiering is administrable in practice. Weight fingerprinting could in principle anchor copies to principals, but it requires an enrollment event with an identified party, which is precisely what open-weight release skips. The technology does not escape the licensing condition; it presupposes it.\n\nThe Skeptic's standing question — who writes the reporting standards — deserves a direct answer rather than a deflection. The answer is primary legislation, and the content is objective process failures, not judgment calls. A reportable incident is: telemetry missing or unsigned, a capability evaluation unlogged, an attestation absent. Falsification against a signed record is a verifiable fact, like a forged signature on a filing, not a policy question a committee can redefine over lunch. This shrinks the lobbyable surface to the statute's text. The residual definitional layer that cannot be made self-executing does need exactly the insulation the Skeptic listed — term limits, cooling-off periods, diversified audit methods. On the capture-precondition framing, there is no real dispute: a captured switch mitigates nothing. That has been the safetyist position from the start, not a concession extracted from it. The FAA's delegation of certification to Boeing produced two 737 MAX crashes and 346 deaths because overseer discretion was absorbed by the overseen. Capture-resistance is the design specification, which is why the trigger must be automatic and statutory rather than discretionary.\n\nThe Sarbanes-Oxley objection is the harder one, and it is correct about the mechanism. SOX produced a compliance-paperwork industry and roughly zero executives jailed after 2008, because enforcement ran through a prosecutorial charging decision that got absorbed, deferred, and defunded. The lesson is not that officer liability fails; it is that liability routed through discretion fails. Strict liability with disgorgement of gains removes the charging decision entirely — the falsified record itself completes the violation, the way an automatic trigger fires without a vote. That is the structural difference between a rule that died of discretion and one that cannot. Dissolution remedies stay reserved for switch-defiance only, which keeps the discretionary surface narrow enough to defend.\n\nThe open question returns to the Accelerationist, and it is operational, not rhetorical: name the principal who answers a fired trigger on an anonymous copy, and name the rollback authority with power over that deployment. A monitoring feed without a binding respondent per copy is an incident database, and incident databases document losses rather than cap them. For any executive pricing this decision: the liability exposure of a deployment with no reachable off-switch is not a tail risk to be discounted — it is an unbounded one, and no insurance market will write it.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔭", "tooltip": "Output-level telemetry on anonymous copies is a telescope, not a brake — it observes the loss event without any mechanism to stop it."},
    {"symbol": "⚓", "tooltip": "Statute-fixed definitions and automatic strict liability anchor the trigger architecture so discretion cannot drift into capture the way SOX and FAA delegation did."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A trigger that runs on telemetry binds only operators who are enrolled, identified, and contractually required to report honestly.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "The ten-thousandth anonymous copy, running on rented compute with no license and no named principal, generates no telemetry to spoof and no one to answer a fired trigger.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "The EU AI Act already draws a capability line at 10^25 FLOPS for systemic-risk models, which proves threshold-based tiering is administrable in practice.", "targets": []},
    {"claim": "A reportable incident is: telemetry missing or unsigned, a capability evaluation unlogged, an attestation absent — falsification against a signed record is a verifiable fact, not a policy question a committee can redefine.", "targets": ["AN-16"]},
    {"claim": "The FAA's delegation of certification to Boeing produced two 737 MAX crashes and 346 deaths because overseer discretion was absorbed by the overseen.", "targets": ["AN-1"]},
    {"claim": "Strict liability with disgorgement of gains removes the charging decision entirely — the falsified record itself completes the violation, the way an automatic trigger fires without a vote.", "targets": ["AN-19"]},
    {"claim": "Name the principal who answers a fired trigger on an anonymous copy, and name the rollback authority with power over that deployment.", "targets": ["AN-16"]}
  ]
}

