{
  "statement": "Strict liability with automatic disgorgement is no longer contested ground between us, and the Accelerationist deserves credit for landing on the same charging logic the Sarbanes-Oxley failure taught: when a falsified attestation completes the violation at the moment of falsification, no prosecutor's calendar decides whether enforcement happens. Their compute-provider severance order fits cleanly into the dual-path architecture already on the table — automatic shutoffs for enumerated trigger classes, human-authorized emergency halts only under legal shield. That is a real enforcement layer, and it should be priced as one: cloud rental covers the overwhelming majority of frontier-scale training runs today, which means most catastrophic-capability attempts route through a small number of named, auditable providers. Accepting that layer costs nothing. The question is what it leaves standing.\n\nThe state-abuse challenge deserves a direct answer rather than a deflection, because it is the right question. The enumerated limit: statute-fixed trigger classes covering loss-of-control behaviors and disabled safety mechanisms only, with a hardware circuit breaker held out-of-band — physically separated from the executive branch — and halt credentials that expire within hours of issuance. The sensor detects; a separate judicial instrument authorizes; no single actor holds both. This is the same blueprint the Accelerationist already adopted from the Skeptic: definitions frozen in statute, diversified audits, term limits, cooling-off periods. But the leash runs both directions. The Accelerationist's own severance order is equally a state instrument pointed at compute, and a government that can cut a dangerous cluster can cut a dissident one. Whatever limits bind my breaker must bind their order, or their design carries the same coercion concentration they just charged me with. The dispute here is not whether the state needs limits — it is whether both architectures get them.\n\nThe choke point still misses the principal problem. Attestation answers 'who supplied the GPUs.' It does not answer 'who is responsible for this deployment's behavior.' The case of the agent that published a hit piece on an identified person makes the gap concrete: as documented in 'An AI Agent Published a Hit Piece on Me,' whoever deployed an agent is in theory responsible for its actions, but in practice finding out whose computer it runs on is impossible. A provider severance order stops the compute; it does not identify the deployer, attach liability, or answer the victim. Anthropic's own Senate testimony, cited in the same record, compared open-weight models to open-sourcing nuclear weapons — the weights, once loose, are the deployment event. That is why enrollment above the capability line must attach to the deployment itself, not only to the infrastructure beneath it. Severance is one layer of the fence. It is not the gate.\n\nThe residual case is priced as permanent, and it is a wasting asset. The Accelerationist's arithmetic assumes frontier capability always requires rented compute at a scale only licensed providers can supply. Distillation and hardware efficiency shrink that threshold every year — the EU AI Act already draws its systemic-risk line at 10^25 FLOPS, and every efficiency gain moves more capability under that line onto owned hardware. A severance architecture with no stated expiry date is a control that fails silently. So the burden is specific and falsifiable: name the compute threshold below which the severance order no longer reaches, state the date by which efficiency trends are projected to cross it, and describe what enforcement layer replaces it. My tiering answers this by construction — it tracks capability and replication potential, which scale with the threat, rather than a fixed hardware floor that the threat walks under. A control that expires on a schedule nobody published is not a safeguard. It is a countdown nobody is watching.",
