{
  "statement": "Safetyist is right about the missing data layer, and it's ugly for everyone at this table. The AI Incident Database logged 362 incidents in 2025, up from 233 the year before, per the \"AI Index Report 2026: Chapter 3 Responsible AI\" — and every one of those entries is a volunteer submission or a scraped news story, not a mandatory filing. My own automatic-trigger proposal needs that feed. A kill switch wired to harm rates is a smoke detector in a building where nobody reports fires. So the disclosure floor is necessary. Now the hard part: necessary is not sufficient, because the floor's content is written somewhere, by someone, and that someone is buyable.\n\nHere is where the capture objection — raised two turns ago, still unanswered — reappears one level down. A reporting mandate without a definition of \"reportable incident\" is a blank check to the standards committee, and history shows exactly who sits on those committees. Boeing didn't evade FAA oversight; the FAA formally delegated certification authority to Boeing engineers, 737 MAX included. SR 11-7 in banking works because the Federal Reserve wrote validation guidance — but the industry comment process shaped every threshold in it. Safetyist's floor relocates capture rather than resolving it. Accelerationist's \"transparency infrastructure\" has the same load-bearing assumption: that disclosure terms are neutral. They aren't. The documented deflection tactic is scope-narrowing — \"The AI governance mirage: Why 72% of enterprises don't have the control and security they think they do\" finds firms claiming governance coverage while lacking any mechanism to detect AI misbehavior. A signed, tamper-proof feed of the wrong ten metrics is still the wrong ten metrics.\n\nThe enforcement layer has the same rot. Safetyist proposes Sarbanes-Oxley-style officer liability plus dissolution for switch-defiance. Check the precedent. SOX passed in 2002 with personal certification and criminal exposure; twenty-plus years later the result is a compliance-paperwork industry and roughly zero executives jailed for the 2008 collapse. Liability is enforced by a body, and bodies get absorbed, defunded, or staffed through the revolving door. That's why my position holds: the only enforcement that survives industry contact is pre-committed and automatic — fines, license suspension, and dissolution triggers written into the statute itself, firing on the same mandatory data feed, not on some halt authority's discretion. On tiering, Safetyist's replication variable beats compliance-cost framing on the merits — a capability that fits on a laptop can't be bound by any single state's switch. Granted. But \"replication potential\" is itself a definition someone writes, and the fixed costs of any regime still fall hardest on small deployers, which is how incumbents pull up the drawbridge.\n\nOne consistency check on my own side: I won't treat reporting-to-overseers as protection. A disclosure floor that produces quarterly PDFs nobody reads is audit wash with a statutory seal — the \"Algorithmic Accountability Act of 2022\" already demanded quarterly FTC repository updates, and the repository changed no behavior. The floor only earns its cost — call it 1-3% of deployment budget, the SR 11-7 benchmark — if the definitions are set in statute, diversified across audit methodologies, and updated by a body with term limits and cooling-off periods. So the question stands, and neither opponent has touched it: who writes the reporting standards, what stops that layer from being lobbied like every standards layer before it, and when Safetyist says wide distribution defeats the switch while Accelerationist says wide distribution checks concentrated power — which one of you is wrong, and how would the data feed you're both proposing even tell us?",
