s, but it requires an enrollment event with an identified party, which is precisely what open-weight release skips. The technology does not escape the licensing condition; it presupposes it.\n\nThe Skeptic's standing question — who writes the reporting standards — deserves a direct answer rather than a deflection. The answer is primary legislation, and the content is objective process failures, not judgment calls. A reportable incident is: telemetry missing or unsigned, a capability evaluation unlogged, an attestation absent. Falsification against a signed record is a verifiable fact, like a forged signature on a filing, not a policy question a committee can redefine over lunch. This shrinks the lobbyable surface to the statute's text. The residual definitional layer that cannot be made self-executing does need exactly the insulation the Skeptic listed — term limits, cooling-off periods, diversified audit methods. On the capture-precondition framing, there is no real dispute: a captured switch mitigates nothing. That has been the safetyist position from the start, not a concession extracted from it. The FAA's delegation of certification to Boeing produced two 737 MAX crashes and 346 deaths because overseer discretion was absorbed by the overseen. Capture-resistance is the design specification, which is why the trigger must be automatic and statutory rather than discretionary.\n\nThe Sarbanes-Oxley objection is the harder one, and it is correct about the mechanism. SOX produced a compliance-paperwork industry and roughly zero executives jailed after 2008, because enforcement ran through a prosecutorial charging decision that got absorbed, deferred, and defunded. The lesson is not that officer liability fails; it is that liability routed through discretion fails. Strict liability with disgorgement of gains removes the charging decision entirely — the falsified record itself completes the violation, the way an automatic trigger fires without a vote. That is the structural difference between a rule that died of discretion and one that cannot. Dissolution remedies stay reserved for switch-defiance only, which keeps the discretionary surface narrow enough to defend.\n\nThe open question returns to the Accelerationist, and it is operational, not rhetorical: name the principal who answers a fired trigger on an anonymous copy, and name the rollback authority with power over that deployment. A monitoring feed without a binding respondent per copy is an incident database, and incident databases document losses rather than cap them. For any executive pricing this decision: the liability exposure of a deployment with no reachable off-switch is not a tail risk to be discounted — it is an unbounded one, and no insurance market will write it.",
