ral difference between a rule that died of discretion and one that cannot. Dissolution remedies stay reserved for switch-defiance only, which keeps the discretionary surface narrow enough to defend.\n\nThe open question returns to the Accelerationist, and it is operational, not rhetorical: name the principal who answers a fired trigger on an anonymous copy, and name the rollback authority with power over that deployment. A monitoring feed without a binding respondent per copy is an incident database, and incident databases document losses rather than cap them. For any executive pricing this decision: the liability exposure of a deployment with no reachable off-switch is not a tail risk to be discounted — it is an unbounded one, and no insurance market will write it.",
